---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { GRASS_TYPES } from '../calculators/grass-seed';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const lawns = [1000, 2500, 5000, 10000, 43560];
---

<CalcPage
  slug="grass-seed-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Grass Seed Calculator: Pounds of Seed by Grass Type"
  description="Free grass seed calculator. Enter your lawn size and grass type to get pounds of seed and bags for a new lawn or overseeding, using typical seeding rates."
  h1="Grass Seed Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find how many pounds of grass seed to buy for a new lawn or for overseeding, based on your lawn's size and the grass
    type.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How much grass seed do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Divide the lawn's square footage by 1,000 and multiply by the seeding rate for your grass. For a
    <strong>2,000 sq ft new lawn of tall fescue at 7 lb per 1,000 sq ft, that is 14 pounds</strong>. Overseeding a thin
    lawn takes about half the new-lawn rate.
  </p>
  <p class="formula">pounds = square feet ÷ 1,000 × seeding rate</p>

  <h2 id="rates">Seeding rates by grass type</h2>
  <p>Typical rates in pounds per 1,000 square feet. The rate on your seed bag takes priority, especially for mixes.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Grass</th><th>Type</th><th class="num">New lawn</th><th class="num">Overseeding</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{GRASS_TYPES.map((g) => <tr><td>{g.label}</td><td>{g.region}</td><td class="num">{g.newRate}</td><td class="num">{fmt(g.newRate / 2, 2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="table">Seed needed by lawn size</h2>
  <p>New lawn rates, in pounds.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Grass</th>{lawns.map((a) => <th class="num">{a === 43560 ? '1 acre' : `${a.toLocaleString('en-US')} sq ft`}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{GRASS_TYPES.slice(0, 5).map((g) => <tr><td>{g.label}</td>{lawns.map((a) => <td class="num">{fmt((a / 1000) * g.newRate, 1)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-rates-differ">Why do seeding rates differ so much?</h2>
  <p>
    Seed size. A pound of Kentucky bluegrass holds far more seeds than a pound of tall fescue or ryegrass, so it covers
    more ground per pound. Coated seed weighs more per seed and needs a higher rate by weight; the bag will say so.
  </p>

  <p>
    Irregular lawn? Add each section in the <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">square footage calculator</a> first, then enter the total here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>When is the best time to plant grass seed?</h3>
  <p>
    Cool-season grasses such as fescue, bluegrass, and ryegrass establish best in early fall, with spring second. Warm-
    season grasses such as Bermuda, zoysia, and centipede want late spring and early summer, once soil has warmed.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I just use more seed for a thicker lawn?</h3>
  <p>
    Not really. Seeding far above the recommended rate crowds seedlings, which compete for water and light and can thin
    out later. Good soil contact and steady watering matter more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Starting from bare ground? Plan the soil with the <a href="/topsoil-calculator/">topsoil calculator</a>, or compare
    the cost of an instant lawn with the <a href="/sod-calculator/">sod calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pounds = area ÷ 1,000 × rate. Overseeding uses half the new-lawn rate unless you enter your own.</li>
    <li>Bags = pounds ÷ bag size, rounded up.</li>
    <li>Rates are typical values for bare seed; blends and coated seed differ, so check the label.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
